We're gonna get into the training and the mindset,

32
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.500
and all of it, but you've got an unbelievable backstory.

33
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.870
So do you mind stepping it back a little bit?

34
00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:22.740
No, I'm happy to.

35
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.573
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

36
00:01:23.573 --> 00:01:25.770
So your upbringing is pretty unique,

37
00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:30.090
and I think there's a lot to be mined, and discussed about,

38
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.000
like how those experiences kind of forged

39
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.260
how you think about sports, and mentality, et cetera today.

40
00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.570
Yeah, when I was growing up to me,

41
00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.270
all my heroes were athletes.

42
00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.940
And it was like my parents were divorced,

43
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.303
so I lived with my mom and my stepfather.

44
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.200
My dad would come and get me a couple times a week

45
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.910
to go play youth hockey.

46
00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.970
So I was like very competitive athlete as a kid,

47
00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.980
played with a bunch of guys that played in the NHL,

48
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.560
Keith Tkachuk, the Saku brother, Shane McGuckin,

49
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.890
like legitimate NHL superstars that were my peers.

50
00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.287
But then in high school they just got better, and I didn't.

51
00:02:07.287 --> 00:02:09.360
And I was like, "Man, what, we're doing the same thing?

52
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.920
How are they getting so much better?"

53
00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.900
But that was my outlet, and those were my heroes.

54
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.450
So I was big, big into sports,

55
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.013
and going through life like that,

56
00:02:21.705 --> 00:02:25.020
it was two very different worlds,

57
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.610
when I would go with my dad to play hockey,

58
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:27.750
and then when I would be home,

59
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.750
we lived with my mother's mom, and her brother,

60
00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.860
who was a lifelong heroin addict, so there was

61
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.060
like completely unstable household people junkies

62
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.730
around all the time, it was very uneasy.

63
00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.280
Yeah, a lot of chaos, your parents got divorced.

64
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.230
So you had your dad, you had a stepdad.

65
00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:52.320
And kind of as I gather sort of upper lower class,

66
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.110
or lower middle class type situation,

67
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:58.710
basically, like blue collar upbringing in Boston.

68
00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.140
My only point of frame of reference for that

69
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.363
is whatever I see in movies, you know what I mean?

70
00:03:03.363 --> 00:03:04.410
It's pretty accurate.

71
00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:07.230
But I can imagine what that must have been like,

72
00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.983
like the chaos of that, and then you like,

73
00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:13.440
what I've seen is somebody who's been in recovery

74
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.673
for a long time is it's sort of that child of alcoholics,

75
00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.530
you're driven to kind of create control amidst the chaos,

76
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.720
and sports was one thing that you could kind

77
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.690
of have domain over, that would be like your thing.

78
00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.660
That's right, and I had such contempt

79
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.660
for drug addicts, and just like unethical people,

80
00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.100
and just like the behavior that was going on around me.

81
00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.690
I had no point of reference to know

82
00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.790
that these people were effed up, but I knew that

83
00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.708
I was not comfortable in this environment.

84
00:03:43.708 --> 00:03:46.110
I knew I didn't belong there

85
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:49.560
to the point where my own family, and people around me

86
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.260
would be like, You think you're better than us?

87
00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.093
You're arrogant.

88
00:03:53.093 --> 00:03:55.299
And I, in my mind, I was like, yeah, I do.

89
00:03:55.299 --> 00:03:57.780
I'm not supposed to be here, I'm not living like this,

90
00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.730
this is constant chaos, like, I don't want this,

91
00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:03.390
I want stability, and I want things in my life.

92
00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.720
I don't wanna aspire to get approved for section eight.

93
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.270
I don't wanna aspire to get like an extra $100

94
00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.983
in food stamps, you know what I mean?

95
00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:13.500
Like my mother sending me to the store with food stamps

96
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.160
was mortifying, all I wanted to do was fit in with the kids

97
00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.330
who had a stable family, and for reference,

98
00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.100
I mean, there was families in the neighborhood

99
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.560
where the dad might have been a firefighter, or a mailman,

100
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.260
and the moms were homemakers, but to me those were like

101
00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.480
the highly successful families.

102
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.183
So in my house it was like,

103
00:04:34.593 --> 00:04:37.353
what perk are we gonna get approved for this month?

104
00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.710
And again, I had no point of reference,

105
00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.990
but I was like, come on, man, don't we wanna aspire

106
00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.360
for more than this, this is embarrassing.

107
00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.340
Like, I don't want anything for free.

108
00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.220
And I had a paper out from as young as I could remember,

109
00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.770
like getting up early, folding the newspapers,

110
00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.470
delivering them in the snow, just because I was like

111
00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:56.680
I wanna have things in my life.

112
00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.860
Yeah, you gotta take care of your own shit, right?

113
00:04:58.860 --> 00:04:59.693
Exactly.

114
00:04:59.693 --> 00:05:03.420
And were you able to seek out mentors in your coaches,

115
00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.040
or teachers, or anything like that?

116
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.190
Like how did you find parenting outside the house?

117
00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:09.330
No, that's a good question.

118
00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.900
I don't necessarily remember having mentors per se,

119
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.390
but when I was about 10 years old,

120
00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.010
I may be getting the years off by a couple,

121
00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.420
but I was about 10 years old,

122
00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.630
and my brother had mental health issues in hindsight,

123
00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.940
and then eventually became a heroin addict.

124
00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.400
But we were like, I was 10, let's say he was nine,

125
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.660
and my mother had signed us up

126
00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.840
for our first sleep away camp, but it was like welfare camp.

127
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:34.080
So we go downtown Boston, and we get on the school bus,

128
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.620
and it's like, Boston was a very racist, segregated place,

129
00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.930
so it was like, maybe they were like 10 white kids,

130
00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.769
and like 50 black kids, which is fine.

131
00:05:41.769 --> 00:05:43.560
I'm painting a picture for you.

132
00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.783
It was like an inner city like camp.

133
00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:48.000
And it was intimidating.

134
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.440
So we get there and I was like,

135
00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.360
I'm going to jail for two weeks.

136
00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.910
I'm nervous, it's very unsettling.

137
00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.256
So we get there, my brother is a maniac,

138
00:05:55.256 --> 00:05:57.750
so he takes off running in in downtown,

139
00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.360
they can't find him, so the school bus leaves without him.

140
00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.970
So now I'm on my way to like prison camp.

141
00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:07.770
And so I get there and it was a lot of sports involved.

142
00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.440
It was like an all sports camp that happened to be the theme

143
00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:11.910
for this two weeks.

144
00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.880
So my mother eventually shows up like a day or two later.

145
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.530
And it's way up, maybe in Maine,

146
00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:17.850
I don't even remember it was so,

147
00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.640
but I just remembered distinctly them showing up,

148
00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.560
and calling me into the camp counselor's office,

149
00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.300
and they're like, "Hey, your mom's here."

150
00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.650
I come in there, I'm like, "Oh, what the fuck?

151
00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:26.850
I don't wanna be involved in this,

152
00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:28.560
I just wanna be left alone,

153
00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.420
I'm serving my sentence, leave me alone.

154
00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:33.180
I'm doing my time, I'll stand on my head for two weeks.

155
00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.030
It's supposed to be this uplifting, nourishing experience.

156
00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.470
No, but it was like a two week vacation

157
00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.870
for everyone's parents to get rid of their kids.

158
00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:40.830
That's what it felt like.

159
00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:41.977
So I get there, my mother's like,

160
00:06:41.977 --> 00:06:43.470
"Hey, listen, he doesn't wanna be here,

161
00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:44.610
your brother doesn't wanna be here,

162
00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.860
you guys can all leave if you want.

163
00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.010
And I was like, "Nah, I'll stay, I'm do my time."

164
00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.170
I don't know why, even at a young age, I was like,

165
00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:55.320
I'm not quit, I'm here, I'm already settled in,

166
00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.450
I've got all my stuff locked up on the locking key,

167
00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.300
I'm sleeping with the keys to my suitcase,

168
00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.940
my hockey bag, which has my all my clothes,

169
00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:05.340
with a lock on it, and the keys are in my pillow case

170
00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.687
because soon as I got there, one of the kids said,

171
00:07:07.687 --> 00:07:09.990
"Hey, let me get those converse you had on.

172
00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:13.380
And I was like, "Yeah, no, you don't have my sneakers."

173
00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.140
So then I'd put them in the bag, lock the bag,

174
00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.790
take the pillows, take the keys,

175
00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:19.170
put them in the pillow case.

176
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:20.317
So when they came and said,

177
00:07:20.317 --> 00:07:22.650
"You're out, you're getting an early release."

178
00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.657
I was like, "Nah, I'll just do my time here."

179
00:07:24.657 --> 00:07:27.420
And I end up winning like camper of the year,

180
00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:28.980
every single award you could win there.

181
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.080
Because I think that in hindsight,

182
00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.790
the leaders must have recognized like,

183
00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:37.020
oh, this kid has something different

184
00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.100
than the other people here.

185
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.640
Like the birth of commitment, discipline, dedication,

186
00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.520
seeing it through, and then being rewarded for that.

187
00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.910
Probably cemented some kind of neural pathway for you.

188
00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.430
Like, oh, this is how you make your way forward.

189
00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:54.450
Yeah, I was like all I can do is like represent myself

190
00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.240
the way I want people to view me,

191
00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.280
and we'll get into it later.

192
00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.080
So then having to deal with my own addiction issues

193
00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:06.543
just brought everything full circle of like man.

194
00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.980
But being back in high school, and then realizing

195
00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:14.646
the NHL is probably not your path, where's your head at

196
00:08:14.646 --> 00:08:18.060
in terms of what you think you're gonna do with your life?

197
00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.220
Yeah, it was around this time

198
00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:21.840
where I like really lost my way

199
00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.450
is when I went to college, I played football and hockey

200
00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:25.563
at Framingham state.

201
00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:31.200
The week after I graduated high school,

202
00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.860
I started my first like full-time summer job as a guard

203
00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.360
in a maximum security prison.

204
00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.850
And one of the coaches that I had played against

205
00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.610
in high school kind of sought me out after the game,

206
00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.990
and said, "What are you gonna do for work?"

207
00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:47.137
He knew my dad, and he said,

208
00:08:47.137 --> 00:08:48.600
"I've got a job for you at the prison."

209
00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.700
And I was like, "Oh cool, man, I'm gonna be cutting grass

210
00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:52.590
and stuff like maintenance."

211
00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:53.820
I showed up there and they hands me

212
00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.400
like basically police uniforms.

213
00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.040
And they give us like a half a day of classroom training,

214
00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.900
like look for this, they put a knife here,

215
00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:02.820
I mean, it was like, this is insane.

216
00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.108
This is day of training to be a prison guard?

217
00:09:05.108 --> 00:09:07.110
I'm telling you, there's so much information coming,

218
00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.450
I'm like, holy, I thought we were cutting grass

219
00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:12.420
when I got here, now we're like guards in a prison?

220
00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.730
I mean, I had known when I was a kid

221
00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:16.350
my stepfather got sent to prison

222
00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:20.070
which was fucking mortifying, as like fifth, sixth grader,

223
00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:23.460
he was like, oh, he's going to prison.

224
00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:24.310
What did he do?

225
00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:29.610
Petty theft and crap, like I think credit card scams.

226
00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.140
But I was just like, man, come on,

227
00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:32.517
why is this happening to me?

228
00:09:32.517 --> 00:09:34.860
You guys can't behave yourself,

229
00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.450
I'm I'm gonna be outta here soon,

230
00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.280
I was just mortified.

231
00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:41.310
This is in like fifth or sixth grade, roughly.

232
00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:45.840
So then around maybe ninth grade, he gets out of prison,

233
00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.370
and he's a nice enough guy, he's just, he is what he is,

234
00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:51.903
I'm not gonna like go scorch that on him.

235
00:09:51.903 --> 00:09:53.430
He's nice enough.

236
00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:56.493
He was doing the best he could I mean, my mother was crazy.

237
00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:01.410
Everyone was in a bad situation, so he gets out of prison,

238
00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.950
and like, hey, he's got a new job, and I'm like, okay, cool,

239
00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:06.780
I could care less.

240
00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.700
He's a janitor, all right, cool.

241
00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.080
At your high school.

242
00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.530
I was like, come on, stop, stop.

243
00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:17.190
I was like, "Please you're joking with me, right?"

244
00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:18.440
And they're like, "Nope."

245
00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.090
The good news is you couldn't do anything

246
00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:25.080
more embarrassing to me.

247
00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.330
Did you get bullied for that?

248
00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:28.680
I never really got bullied 'cause

249
00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:32.160
I wasn't like a pushover, but I certainly like would imagine

250
00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.710
that even if it wasn't true that people were talking me

251
00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.900
behind my back, but there was a part of me too

252
00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.090
that was like, I can't control the actions of other people.

253
00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:42.930
I had had a brother who had been kicked out of every school,

254
00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:44.958
gotten into fights, wasn't an athlete was just like

255
00:10:44.958 --> 00:10:49.487
a bad kid, which was always constantly haunting me.

256
00:10:49.487 --> 00:10:53.370
This was just another drop in the bucket of like,

257
00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.560
come on man, which is part of the reason why I was like,

258
00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.720
I gotta get outta here.

259
00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:59.350
So you take this job as a prison guard?

260
00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.440
Yeah, so I start working in the prison,

261
00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.560
walk in there the first day, and this big Irish gangster

262
00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:06.720
from the streets who I knew from the projects comes running,

263
00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:07.920
and picks me up on his shoulder,

264
00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:09.810
he's running around with me in the prison yard.

265
00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:12.150
And I was thinking like, dude, come on, man, pull me down,

266
00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:13.080
the other guards are watching.

267
00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:14.910
And I didn't care about the inmates I was like,

268
00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:16.920
I don't wanna the guards to think I'm like fraternizing

269
00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.600
with the inmates first day.

270
00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.700
But it was almost his way of saying like, no, no, no,

271
00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.610
I'm letting everyone know you're with us like we know you.

272
00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:26.370
So like this is a dangerous place, people should know that

273
00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.410
you know people here, it's just like being an inmate.

274
00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.930
If you go to prison, it's very segregated,

275
00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.700
if you don't know the people in your race,

276
00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.280
has being a street person, you're gonna have a problem,

277
00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.170
'cause they're all gonna want to have a problem with you.

278
00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:39.480
Right, like they're gonna protect you as opposed

279
00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:40.713
to the other way around.

280
00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.088
I mean, were you 17, 18 Years old?

281
00:11:43.088 --> 00:11:44.520
Not necessarily protect but then.

282
00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:45.563
I was 18 just outta high school.

283
00:11:45.563 --> 00:11:48.390
I mean, I looked like I was probably 13.

284
00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:50.568
It was crazy.

285
00:11:50.568 --> 00:11:53.250
It wasn't as crazy in reality as it sounds now

286
00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:57.153
as we sit here talking in Malibu, but at the time,

287
00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.660
I grew up with most of these people.

288
00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:04.020
I mean, these were the bad people, but I knew a lot of them.

289
00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:06.780
So when I got there, it was kind of like,

290
00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:07.613
Hey, what's on?

291
00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.370
And what was the day to day experience

292
00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:13.050
of working there like?

293
00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.210
It was the opposite of what you think of with

294
00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:16.290
as shushing redemption,

295
00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:20.302
it was a very hostile, intimidating place.

296
00:12:20.302 --> 00:12:21.780
If an inmate had a problem with you,

297
00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.880
they'd punch you right in the face, they wouldn't hesitate,

298
00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:27.210
they demanded respect within reason,

299
00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:28.950
the same that I would demand from them is like,

300
00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:30.630
you'd show mutual respect.

301
00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:33.570
And it's always like imagine being a substitute teacher

302
00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:35.160
at a high school full of fresh little kids,

303
00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:38.410
and they're always testing you, and they're doing things,

304
00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:40.200
you're not gonna really like come down hard on them,

305
00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.060
but it's kind of like, we're getting close

306
00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:42.987
to the line here of where.

307
00:12:42.987 --> 00:12:44.970
And you can't back down?
No.

308
00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:48.510
And you being this young kid going face to face

309
00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:52.080
with guys, hardened criminals guys, much older than you.

310
00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:54.150
But I always tell people just because you're in jail

311
00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:56.580
being stupid doesn't make you tough.

312
00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:59.310
There's tough people, there's tough people in Malibu,

313
00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:01.500
there's tough people in the private schools,

314
00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:04.110
public schools, prisons, tough is tough.

315
00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:05.910
And that's the one thing I've learned over the years,

316
00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:09.270
in terms of like kind of vacillate

317
00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:11.910
in between these different worlds is that tough is tough.

318
00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:13.230
You mess with the wrong person,

319
00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.450
you're gonna have a long day ahead of you.

320
00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:19.170
But yeah, the inmates, they weren't necessarily all tough,

321
00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:21.480
but they were all pretty crazy.

322
00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.200
Did you end up in violent altercations there at all?

323
00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:26.250
Oh yeah, multiple times I got hit over the head

324
00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:27.690
with a pillow case that had a weight

325
00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:30.267
in it once, fractured my skull, an inmate said,

326
00:13:30.267 --> 00:13:31.590
'If you didn't have that badge on,

327
00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:32.670
I'd punch you right in your face."

328
00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:33.720
I said, "Oh really?"

329
00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:35.700
And I went to take the badge off, and he punched me right

330
00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:37.431
in the face before I even put my hands on it.

331
00:13:37.431 --> 00:13:38.363
(both laughs)

332
00:13:38.363 --> 00:13:40.672
My God, how long did you work there?

333
00:13:40.672 --> 00:13:43.590
Three summers, three or four summers.

334
00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:46.380
But there was one incident where I was working there

335
00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:47.970
with a friend of mine called Brendan Daley.

336
00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.910
He he eventually became a singer called Spooky Daley,

337
00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:54.120
and he's a director, and producer now real very successful.

338
00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.330
But him and I were working in the like modular building,

339
00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:59.640
which that's the main building, which has like cells,

340
00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:00.473
like you would think,

341
00:14:00.473 --> 00:14:01.740
and then there's like modular buildings,

342
00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.390
which were more like dormitories.

343
00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:06.720
So inmate came in, and some of the other inmates

344
00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:07.553
would teasing them.

345
00:14:07.553 --> 00:14:09.300
Again, it's not like a movie,

346
00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:10.920
it's like a high school full of jerks.

347
00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:13.290
And they're bullying kids, it's just like that.

348
00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:15.510
So kid comes in, so they're teasing him, and telling him

349
00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:18.150
they're gonna get him and stuff, and he's new.

350
00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:21.300
And they send him down to the warden's office,

351
00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:22.380
the warden of the dormitory,

352
00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.060
so the guy, superintendent of the dorms.

353
00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:25.620
And he calls me and Brendan now.

354
00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:29.610
And we're like the like dopey summer help,

355
00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.500
that they hired all these college kids

356
00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.720
so that the full-time guys could take summer vacations,

357
00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.430
and they wouldn't be short staffed.

358
00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:36.870
So they call us down, and we go down there,

359
00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:39.300
and the poor kid, he's a little bit older than me,

360
00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:41.340
a little bit bigger than me and my friend, Brandon.

361
00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:45.600
And he's in a position now where he's not gonna tell

362
00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.550
on them, 'cause you're not gonna be a rat,

363
00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.590
like that's gonna change the trajectory of your time.

364
00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.543
You're gonna go to protective custody.

365
00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:55.140
But he also is now in with the superintendent,

366
00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:57.090
like everyone's gonna be watching

367
00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:58.260
how this whole thing plays out.

368
00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:01.020
So he's realizing, shit I gotta do something.

369
00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:03.360
So to save face, he picks something up off the desk

370
00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:04.447
and half heartedly says to the guy,

371
00:15:04.447 --> 00:15:05.760
"I'll bash your head in with this."

372
00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:06.847
So then he says to me and Brandon,

373
00:15:06.847 --> 00:15:07.830
"Get this guy outta here."

374
00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:10.020
So we handcuff him, and he's struggling,

375
00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:11.347
and I'm like talking, and I'm like,

376
00:15:11.347 --> 00:15:12.420
"Dude, will you cut the shit?"

377
00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:15.483
Like, dude, stop, let's make this easy.

378
00:15:17.158 --> 00:15:19.530
Now he's like, we're on him, and it's just like a movie.

379
00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:21.720
He's like, "Oh, fuck you, don't hold me back."

380
00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:22.770
I'm like, "Come on."

381
00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:24.090
So we get down to isolation.

382
00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:26.580
The superintendent of the whole prison comes down,

383
00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:27.780
and they're all standing in isolation,

384
00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:29.527
they put him in the cell, and they're like,

385
00:15:29.527 --> 00:15:31.950
"Okay, take your clothes off, strip search."

386
00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:33.990
They always strip search anyone anytime

387
00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:34.950
they move from buildings.

388
00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:38.517
And he says, " I'm not taking my clothes off."

389
00:15:38.517 --> 00:15:39.517
And the guy says to me,

390
00:15:39.517 --> 00:15:41.340
"Ken, get in there, and strip his clothes off."

391
00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:42.930
And now we're like down in a dungeon

392
00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:46.380
in this like fifties era, like bunker.

393
00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.840
And I'm like, oh my God, I can't believe this is happened,

394
00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.370
I'm in a cage match.

395
00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:51.720
I go in, they closed the door,

396
00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:52.890
and I'm like, "Take your clothes off."

397
00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:54.457
And he just stood there for a second, I said,

398
00:15:54.457 --> 00:15:55.440
"This is your last warning."

399
00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.050
'Cause now I'm just like gonna go attack mode.

400
00:15:58.050 --> 00:15:59.790
And he just finally takes his clothes off,

401
00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:02.070
and It's like, oh thank you, Jesus.

402
00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:02.903
And I got out of there.

403
00:16:02.903 --> 00:16:06.345
And no one's coming in unless something goes down

404
00:16:06.345 --> 00:16:07.178
one way or the other.

405
00:16:07.178 --> 00:16:08.070
Oh no this kid's getting a beating,

406
00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:09.990
either, I'm gonna beat him, or they're all gonna come in,

407
00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:10.823
and beat him up.

408
00:16:10.823 --> 00:16:12.510
And he's gonna like, if he's getting the better, I mean,

409
00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:16.050
they're gonna get him, but still I'm still a normal person,

410
00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:18.600
I don't wanna be in a death match in a prison.

411
00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.680
But I use that story as an anecdote for life

412
00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:26.160
because when you think about things that are scary,

413
00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.080
or intimidating like training with someone,

414
00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.030
or running a race that you're nervous about,

415
00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:32.550
or any situation in life you're nervous about,

416
00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.830
especially with running I always tell people this,

417
00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:36.090
no one's gonna punch you in the face,

418
00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:38.580
no one's gonna hurt you If you can't take anymore,

419
00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:40.470
you can just stop whenever you want.

420
00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:42.960
So when you think about that in relation to being

421
00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:46.830
in really intimidating situations, running to me is like,

422
00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:49.773
come on man, this is easy work.

423
00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:54.510
You've said in the past that when you were starting

424
00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.670
to really get into running, you kind of looked around,

425
00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:59.790
and you saw a bunch of skinny guys and you're like,

426
00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:02.760
I was a prison guard, how hard can this be?

427
00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.040
I can out tough these guys.

428
00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:06.240
Just 'cause they're skinny doesn't mean

429
00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:07.653
they're not mentally tough.

430
00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:10.470
You don't have to box.

431
00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:12.423
I say to people all the time, like look at these guys

432
00:17:12.423 --> 00:17:15.450
that like running dweebs, but I count myself in that group.

433
00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:18.540
And I mean it with affection, I don't mean literally,

434
00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:20.550
but it doesn't matter what the reality is,

435
00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:22.080
it's what I tell myself.

436
00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:26.010
So I tell myself, these guys are soft, they're wimps.

437
00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:29.580
That's not the truth, I'm a rational person,

438
00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:30.413
I know that this, like I just said,

439
00:17:30.413 --> 00:17:31.980
there's tough people everywhere,

440
00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:35.520
that the nerdiest looking guy might Jitsu your ass

441
00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:37.500
into submission in three seconds.

442
00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.480
But that doesn't matter, what matters is the story

443
00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:41.970
that I'm telling myself, and I'm telling myself,

444
00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:43.560
I'm 10 times tougher than everyone,

445
00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:44.970
and until someone proves otherwise,

446
00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:47.670
I think I can win the New York Marathon, the whole thing,.

447
00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:49.650
Like I go in with the attitude of that.

448
00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:52.950
Where does that confidence, that brashness come from?

449
00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:54.960
Is that just built into you?

450
00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:57.450
Is that born out of all of these experiences

451
00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:59.040
that you had as a young person?

452
00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.530
I think so, because at the corner of me,

453
00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:06.090
I'm like super, super anxious, insecure, nervous,

454
00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:06.923
just like anyone.

455
00:18:06.923 --> 00:18:10.630
I have all those similar insecurities if not more so that

456
00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.930
I wouldn't say I'm overcompensating because I'm not trying

457
00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:18.300
to run from that, I know that that insecurity,

458
00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.062
and that fear is there.

459
00:18:20.062 --> 00:18:22.860
It's a tactic to overcome that insecurity.

460
00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:25.560
Yeah, and I try to keep the fear in check,

461
00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.230
but I never try to deny it because I tell people

462
00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:30.603
all the time, like someone at one point,

463
00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:34.050
the best fighters in the world, at some point they walked

464
00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:36.330
into a gym for the first time, and was scared shirtless.

465
00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.120
If you walk into a boxing gym, or a fighting gym,

466
00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:41.550
and you don't have experience, and you're not scared,

467
00:18:41.550 --> 00:18:43.230
or nervous, that's not normal.

468
00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:46.020
And anyone who says anything different, I think is lying.

469
00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.020
Well, they're crazy.

470
00:18:50.313 --> 00:18:53.310
An important element of my training and racing,

471
00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:55.560
and the stuff I do with endurance is to acknowledge that

472
00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.870
yeah, I'm scared, I'm nervous,

473
00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.750
and I may just be nervous in disappointing myself,

474
00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:02.433
but I'm nervous and I'm scared.

475
00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:09.060
But those feelings let me know that it's time to get dark.

476
00:19:12.073 --> 00:19:14.880
And indeed, even when it's the Turkey trot.

477
00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:17.910
100% Turkey trot coming for your neck.

478
00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.801
I'm elbowing, forearm, shivering people on the start.

479
00:19:20.801 --> 00:19:21.896
(both laughs)

480
00:19:21.896 --> 00:19:23.256
Oh my God.

481
00:19:23.256 --> 00:19:25.839
(upbeat music)

482
00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:29.940
It's not surprising you somehow found

483
00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:31.140
your way to Wall Street.

484
00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:34.260
Yeah, so.

485
00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:38.160
By hooker, by crook, not by academic pedigree.

486
00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:39.990
No, and to be honest with you,

487
00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:42.270
I can be very self deprecating,

488
00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:43.860
and I'm aware of all my weaknesses

489
00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:45.480
and the mistakes I've made, but there are things

490
00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:47.880
that I've been proud of that I've accomplished

491
00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:48.713
along the way.

492
00:19:48.713 --> 00:19:51.330
And one of those things is going to New York,

493
00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:54.480
and seeing that there was opportunity that kids were working

494
00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:58.173
in finance, and making money and being successful,

495
00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:01.710
same thing with running, I was like, these kids can do it?

496
00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.780
They have fancier degrees that someone told them

497
00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:06.150
about this earlier, but if I can just get

498
00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:08.370
onto a trading desk, and get my foot in the door,

499
00:20:08.370 --> 00:20:11.010
I can kill these guys, I can out hustle these guys,

500
00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:11.913
I know I can.

501
00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:15.480
And when it say it now, it seems crazy,

502
00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:17.130
'cause it almost seems delusional.

503
00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:19.770
At the time I had a sociology degree in Framingham State.

504
00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:22.440
I was working with guys who had Ivy league undergrad,

505
00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:24.810
Ivy league MBA, and now all of a sudden

506
00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:26.550
we were on this same trading desk,

507
00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:28.854
but I'll step back, and kind of explain to you

508
00:20:28.854 --> 00:20:31.170
how I got to New York, and segued into that.

509
00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:35.640
So I got my first job as like pharmaceutical sales job.

510
00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:39.300
It was awful, but I lived in New York and I literally.

511
00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:41.970
Everybody needs that thing that gets them to New York.

512
00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:46.050
Once you're there, the window of possibility opens up.

513
00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:49.080
So I get to New York, and I'm doing pharmaceutical sales,

514
00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:51.360
and pharmaceutical sales is the biggest racket ever.

515
00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:53.280
When I tell you I probably work 30 minutes a day,

516
00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:56.730
that would be aggressive, it wasn't good for me,

517
00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:57.720
I need structure.

518
00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:00.120
And it was like, you're left to your own devices,

519
00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:01.793
and I hated it.

520
00:21:01.793 --> 00:21:03.930
You had to drop in on doctor's offices.

521
00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:06.660
Exactly, drop sample samples, get them to sign.

522
00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:09.270
So it just became an exercise in like dropping the samples,

523
00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:11.850
getting the signatures, and taking off.

524
00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:15.420
I had no direction, I knew I didn't wanna do this.

525
00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:16.920
So I was working out at the gym,

526
00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.870
and playing in a men's league hockey league

527
00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:21.960
in Chelsea Piers, and one of the kids

528
00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.761
who I happened to just play with on a pickup team

529
00:21:24.761 --> 00:21:26.940
was a inter dealer broker.

530
00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:30.540
So he was brokering institutional trades between the banks.

531
00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:32.550
It was kind of like the bottom rank

532
00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:35.130
of the like prestige ladder in finance.

533
00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:37.620
Like inter dealer broker is kind of like the bottom rank

534
00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:39.810
in terms of prestige, but you could make a lot of money,

535
00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:40.643
millions and millions.

536
00:21:40.643 --> 00:21:44.010
There were people getting fabulously wealthy doing it,

537
00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:46.950
I always told people, this was a job, it wasn't a career.

538
00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:48.570
You're only as good as the relationships

539
00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.670
that you have with certain traders that were at banks

540
00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:53.250
that eventually those guys went to hedge funds,

541
00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:56.103
or asset managers, so there was constant turnover.

542
00:21:56.970 --> 00:21:58.468
So I got onto the trading desk,

543
00:21:58.468 --> 00:22:00.000
this is actually a funny story.

544
00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.853
I got onto a trading desk as like the assistant,

545
00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.060
and I was the assistant, so I was covering young guys

546
00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:09.150
at Enron doing like next day power trades which were kind

547
00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:11.670
of like a necessity for the senior traders

548
00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:14.490
to do the bigger trades with your senior brokers.

549
00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:17.070
So the junior people were talking to junior people at Enron.

550
00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:19.080
The trade was like made $10 in commission,

551
00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:22.140
but it was a necessary evil for the electricity traders

552
00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:23.820
that the next day power had to flow,

553
00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:25.560
so they had to trade that, balance their books,

554
00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:26.820
and then if you did a good job there,

555
00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:28.500
they would reward the senior people

556
00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:30.510
with their like real trading.

557
00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:32.190
So I was doing that, and there were like two

558
00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:34.260
or three guys there just hazing me,

559
00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:35.520
which was a whole new experience to me.

560
00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:39.930
I was like, these guys are hazing me like I'm a nerd.

561
00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:41.910
They're being abusive towards me, I was like,

562
00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:44.110
I can't believe I'm allowing this to happen.

563
00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:47.653
Little do they know like you are a prison guard hazing.

564
00:22:47.653 --> 00:22:49.590
I was boxing for the New York Athletic Club,

565
00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:51.600
I had a black guy every other day, they knew.

566
00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:54.990
But they just like, it's just typical finance bro bullshit,

567
00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:56.490
and they were like terrorizing me.

568
00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:58.740
And one day the guy threw a dry eraser at me,

569
00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.270
and I just slapped him in the face.

570
00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:02.880
And I was like, we're not doing this anymore,

571
00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:03.780
you got the wrong guy.

572
00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:05.760
And they were like, you're fired.

573
00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:09.390
I was like, oh my God, when I say I had no money,

574
00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:11.910
I was literally making like 40 grand, but by the time

575
00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:14.010
I paid my rent, my student loans, everything,

576
00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:15.423
I was negative.

577
00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:19.080
So this is so funny.

578
00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:22.230
I called from a payphone, mind you, this is in like 96.

579
00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:23.833
I called the kids at Enron I was like, dude,

580
00:23:23.833 --> 00:23:26.700
I just got fired, I'm telling you what happened.

581
00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:28.770
Coincidentally, the senior trader at Enron,

582
00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:31.260
who was like the Gordon Gecko of commodities at the time

583
00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:32.880
was from Martha's Vineyard.

584
00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:36.270
And he heard the story and was like,

585
00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:37.230
give me that kid's number.

586
00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:40.200
So he calls me and he says, "Hey, I heard what happened,

587
00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:41.550
call this guy at."

588
00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:44.370
I didn't even know we had competitors in what I was doing,

589
00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:45.303
but one of the competitors got in touch with me,

590
00:23:45.303 --> 00:23:47.610
and was like, this was, let's say a Thursday,

591
00:23:47.610 --> 00:23:49.702
he's like, "Can you start Monday?"

592
00:23:49.702 --> 00:23:51.360
And I was like, "Start what?"

593
00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:53.460
He's like, "Come work here on Monday."

594
00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:54.893
I was like, "Okay, I was making 40 grand."

595
00:23:54.893 --> 00:23:57.570
And the guy goes, "How's 80 grand."

596
00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.670
I was like 80 grand?

597
00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:00.960
That was more money than my dad made.

598
00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:02.493
I was like, I'm rich.

599
00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:06.150
That there was something about you slapping

600
00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:07.317
that guy that appealed to him?

601
00:24:07.317 --> 00:24:10.957
Oh, yeah.
This guy is a gunner.

602
00:24:10.957 --> 00:24:11.790
Yeah, he was like, what happened?

603
00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:14.823
And I told him, and he was like, stand by.

604
00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:20.760
Then when I got there, he told the guy who hired me,

605
00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:22.950
that's my broker, so he starts calling me

606
00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:25.370
and saying, buy this, sell that to the guys on the desk.

607
00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:27.390
It's like bad Fox in wall street

608
00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:29.040
when Gordon Gecko starts giving them orders.

609
00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:29.873
Yeah, I'm thing about that.

610
00:24:29.873 --> 00:24:32.100
I'm thinking about the Jordan Bellfort origin story.

611
00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:33.330
That's exactly what it was.

612
00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:36.030
All of a sudden, when I tell you I was making 40 grand

613
00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:37.323
within two or three months, they're like,

614
00:24:37.323 --> 00:24:39.420
your new salary is 125.

615
00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:40.620
Now keep in mind, they keep raising it,

616
00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:42.521
but I'm probably making like three or $4 million

617
00:24:42.521 --> 00:24:45.300
for the trading desk, but I just don't know enough

618
00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:47.220
to even know what to ask for.

619
00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:50.880
To me, it was like, I think they made a mistake, 125 grand,

620
00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:52.110
did they know I'm an idiot?

621
00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.620
Like they're crazy, so I started just grinding away.

622
00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:57.970
And then eventually the guy who hired me there,

623
00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:01.020
he went to Cantor Fitzgerald to run

624
00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:03.367
their electricity business and said,

625
00:25:03.367 --> 00:25:05.037
"Hey, come here, come work here."

626
00:25:05.037 --> 00:25:07.170
And I said, "I have a contract, you signed it,

627
00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:08.550
you know I can't leave.

628
00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:10.320
We have a real contract."

629
00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:12.930
And he is like, "Okay, go to London for a few months,

630
00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:14.250
and then come back."

631
00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.680
So I did, and while I was in London, 9/11 happened.

632
00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:18.120
And all those people that I would've worked with

633
00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:20.910
were on the top floor of the World Trade Center.

634
00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:22.770
But then you went to Cantor after that?

635
00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:25.424
No, no.
So you went to Cantor.

636
00:25:25.424 --> 00:25:27.370
So you were employed by Cantor when 9/11 happened?

637
00:25:27.370 --> 00:25:29.520
And I was going back and forth between London

638
00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:31.800
and New York, I was about to leave for the airport

639
00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:34.140
to go back to New York to work for a few days,

640
00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:35.820
and 9/11 happens.

641
00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:36.660
Yeah, it was crazy.

642
00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:39.029
So obviously you knew a lot of those people.

643
00:25:39.029 --> 00:25:42.363
Oh, a ton, I mean, I worked there a few days a week.

644
00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:45.090
This is how crazy it was,

645
00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:47.370
two years before I was making 40 grand, now I'm flying

646
00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:49.474
the Concord back and forth from London to New York.

647
00:25:49.474 --> 00:25:51.720
And every time I would be like,

648
00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:54.360
I'm living someone else's fantasy, this can't be real.

649
00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:55.860
Unbelievable.

650
00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:57.510
What do the guys back home think?

651
00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:02.220
I think initially people were like,

652
00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:04.950
it's almost like a natural instinct to be like F that guy,

653
00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:07.980
he thinks he's better than us, like crab's in a bucket.

654
00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:10.170
They want you to succeed, but not really.

655
00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:13.710
It's like, if you're half succeeding, if it's very visible

656
00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:16.260
like you're playing in the NHL, they're all like

657
00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:18.270
your biggest fans, they'll kill someone for you.

658
00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:20.460
But if you're kind of just pulling away financially,

659
00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:23.190
there's that crabs in the bucket thing of like,

660
00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:25.350
they don't really wanna see you succeed too much.

661
00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:28.020
Yeah, you're not all that.
Exactly.

662
00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:31.260
And I would say that that has dissipated immensely

663
00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:33.480
since I started doing the podcast with Teddy,

664
00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:36.780
but we can get into that in a minute, but yeah,

665
00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:38.610
when I was doing all this stuff, it was fun.

666
00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:41.430
And of course, I wanted to like share

667
00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:42.330
the wealth with everyone

668
00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:45.450
I was always been maybe overly generous,

669
00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:47.730
and I would always have friends fly down to Boston,

670
00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:49.680
fly down to New York, we'd go to fights,

671
00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:53.880
we'd go to sporting events, in hindsight,

672
00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:58.844
it was all a bit too much, too soon.

673
00:26:58.844 --> 00:27:02.430
And around that time was when I was like really suffering

674
00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:05.010
from like fraud complex, or imposter syndrome,

675
00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:07.690
and was super uncomfortable just with the way

676
00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:09.693
things were going.

677
00:27:10.975 --> 00:27:13.530
I say like the fear of having money and losing it

678
00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:16.230
was 10 times worse than never having money.

679
00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:18.450
That anxiety around not making mistakes

680
00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:21.003
once I had had some financial success,

681
00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:26.880
it was overbearing, and that environment working in finance

682
00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:30.120
at that level was incredibly, incredibly stressful.

683
00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:31.470
Like I said, the relationships

684
00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:35.490
that you maintain dictated essentially your compensation,

685
00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:38.370
and trying to maintain those relationships,

686
00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:42.810
and be a good person without being like a complete ask kiss,

687
00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:46.170
or like just constantly living and dying

688
00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:49.890
with these interactions with other men and women,

689
00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:52.050
it was just a lot of emotional stress

690
00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:53.880
that I wasn't prepared to deal with.

691
00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:56.130
Right, being so young,

692
00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:58.950
and really being inexperienced in the workplace

693
00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:02.310
to get thrown into that pressure cooker

694
00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:06.210
with the imposter syndrome, and diluting yourself

695
00:28:06.210 --> 00:28:10.340
with the belief that the money is gonna take care of all

696
00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:12.450
of those feelings.

697
00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:14.400
And so what happens, you end up compensating

698
00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:15.870
in other unhealthy ways.

699
00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:16.920
That's exactly right.

700
00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:19.710
I wanna get into that, but before I forget,

701
00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:23.580
I do want to ask about what it was like to return

702
00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:28.580
to New York city after 9/11 being a Cantor employee.

703
00:28:28.590 --> 00:28:31.500
I mean, it must have just been devastating.

704
00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:35.070
It was devastating, but again, it wasn't like you had

705
00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:36.570
these relationships with people

706
00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:38.610
that were like lifelong friends or family,

707
00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:41.883
it was like, we were almost like mercenaries together.

708
00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:47.970
It's hard to explain if you've never been on a trading desk.

709
00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:49.380
There were some people that you really like,

710
00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:51.270
and there's some other people you really dislike.

711
00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:53.670
And that's the one thing that I don't miss,

712
00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:55.380
is having conflict with people.

713
00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:58.590
Since I've worked for myself, and segued into the stuff

714
00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:00.210
that I'm doing now that we can get into later,

715
00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:02.790
it's like I've never felt more at peace,

716
00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:05.430
and never had less conflict with people.

717
00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:10.430
And that took a while to come to that.

718
00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:12.180
It took a while to come to that realization

719
00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:13.380
that that was what was going on,

720
00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:17.130
but like going through rehab and recovery,

721
00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:18.570
you kind of like realized these things

722
00:29:18.570 --> 00:29:20.400
that you had never considered before,

723
00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:24.780
but going back to New York after 9/11 happened,

724
00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:25.983
yeah, it was traumatic.

725
00:29:28.409 --> 00:29:31.410
I think some people who had some loose affiliation,

726
00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:35.072
or knew people that were there, it hit them a lot harder,

727
00:29:35.072 --> 00:29:38.550
and they held onto that trauma probably more than I did.

728
00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:41.520
Whereas I was like, it is what it is.

729
00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:45.000
I thank God I wasn't here, this is awful,

730
00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:46.320
but I can't let it consume me,

731
00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:50.463
this is the ups and downs of life.

732
00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:54.360
But yeah, it was difficult.

733
00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:56.610
I mean, we were in the building in London,

734
00:29:56.610 --> 00:29:59.880
and on open calls to the New York.

735
00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:01.380
We had like an open line of communication,

736
00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:03.690
there wasn't video, but you had an open line.

737
00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:05.070
There was a guy sitting next to me,

738
00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:06.810
his brother and his dad were in the building.

739
00:30:06.810 --> 00:30:08.730
This whole thing played out in slow motion.

740
00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:10.200
It wasn't like, oh my God, the building's done.

741
00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:12.480
It was like, oh, someone hit the buildings with,

742
00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:13.920
what is that a Cessna?

743
00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.090
Then the second one hits me like,

744
00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:16.980
oh my God, I hope they can get out of there.

745
00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:19.830
But never like, oh my God, this is this about to get ugly.

746
00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:21.120
And then when the buildings fell down,

747
00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:25.667
then it was like wheels off, chaos.

748
00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:28.830
So you come back to New York.

749
00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:31.530
Yes, I come back to New York back and forth,

750
00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:32.490
and once that happened,

751
00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:34.260
like there was no real office in New York

752
00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:35.880
for 6 to 12 months.

753
00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:38.850
So I was in new London working around the clock trying

754
00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:39.684
to cover for that.

755
00:30:39.684 --> 00:30:40.740
Were you with your wife already at this point?

756
00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:43.980
No, nope, I was single at this point, thank God,

757
00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:45.960
because I still had a lot of mistakes to make,

758
