00:50:59.093 --> 00:51:01.605
And it's pretty busy depending upon time of day.

1253
00:51:01.605 --> 00:51:04.860
And the funny thing is, soon as I jumped up,

1254
00:51:04.860 --> 00:51:07.830
I'm burned up everywhere my back, all my clothes

1255
00:51:07.830 --> 00:51:09.270
are ripped off for the most part,

1256
00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:11.730
and I'm just bleeding everywhere, including my head.

1257
00:51:11.730 --> 00:51:14.640
So I jump up, and my business partner

1258
00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:17.790
who was with me, is the nicest guy, but he's like very,

1259
00:51:17.790 --> 00:51:20.250
he's not timid, but he's like not as aggressive as me.

1260
00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:21.810
So he's like, "Oh my God, oh my God."

1261
00:51:21.810 --> 00:51:23.490
And I'm like, "Dude, how bad is my back?

1262
00:51:23.490 --> 00:51:24.323
It's killing me."

1263
00:51:24.323 --> 00:51:25.830
He's like, "It's bad, it's bad."

1264
00:51:25.830 --> 00:51:27.630
And that kind snapped me out of it 'cause I was like,

1265
00:51:27.630 --> 00:51:30.960
dude, lie to me, tell me it's not that bad even if it is.

1266
00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:34.230
So anyway then the ambulance, fire trucks, everything came,

1267
00:51:34.230 --> 00:51:36.330
and took me out of there.

1268
00:51:36.330 --> 00:51:39.120
Yeah, I've had some serious wipe outs on my bike,

1269
00:51:39.120 --> 00:51:40.680
and my wife would be like, "Why do you keep doing this?"

1270
00:51:40.680 --> 00:51:42.120
I'm like, "That was an anomaly,

1271
00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:44.067
that's never gonna happen again."

1272
00:51:45.060 --> 00:51:47.040
It all starts in New York City,

1273
00:51:47.040 --> 00:51:48.780
New York City in the park.

1274
00:51:48.780 --> 00:51:51.330
And riding and running, and I entered duathlon

1275
00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:53.970
in Central Park, like a two mile run, 12 mile bike,

1276
00:51:53.970 --> 00:51:54.803
two mile run.

1277
00:51:54.803 --> 00:51:56.790
And let's say, I came in like 20th overall,

1278
00:51:56.790 --> 00:51:57.667
and very quickly I was like,

1279
00:51:57.667 --> 00:51:59.430
"Oh my God, I didn't even know what I was doing,

1280
00:51:59.430 --> 00:52:01.167
I bet you, I can win this."

1281
00:52:01.167 --> 00:52:03.210
And of course my wife, who I was dating at the time

1282
00:52:03.210 --> 00:52:04.620
was like, "You really think so?"

1283
00:52:04.620 --> 00:52:07.020
She was kind of somewhat interested,

1284
00:52:07.020 --> 00:52:10.020
but not like obsessed with it like I was.

1285
00:52:10.020 --> 00:52:12.750
And sure enough, I started to do little races,

1286
00:52:12.750 --> 00:52:16.170
the North Fork, sprint triathlon,

1287
00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:18.360
and doing little things here and there, picking up tons

1288
00:52:18.360 --> 00:52:19.650
of tidbits here and there, but keep mind,

1289
00:52:19.650 --> 00:52:21.840
I didn't even know how to swim when I started in triathlon.

1290
00:52:21.840 --> 00:52:23.760
And you're how old at this point?

1291
00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:26.373
I wanna say early to mid thirties,

1292
00:52:28.197 --> 00:52:31.977
maybe around 30, yeah, around 30 to 35.

1293
00:52:31.977 --> 00:52:33.810
And I went to the New York Athletic Club

1294
00:52:33.810 --> 00:52:34.860
where I was a member,

1295
00:52:35.880 --> 00:52:37.920
I knew how to swim if I fell off a boat,

1296
00:52:37.920 --> 00:52:40.800
I could go water skiing, but I wasn't a competitive swimmer.

1297
00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:43.473
So when I'd see guys like you swimming, I'd be like,

1298
00:52:43.473 --> 00:52:46.320
"Oh, how is this guy so much faster than me?"

1299
00:52:46.320 --> 00:52:48.630
It would drive me crazy 'cause it was so much technique.

1300
00:52:48.630 --> 00:52:50.040
'Cause I was like, I have good fitness,

1301
00:52:50.040 --> 00:52:51.660
how are they swimming circles around me?

1302
00:52:51.660 --> 00:52:54.000
Even the guys who are in terrible shape.

1303
00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:55.200
I got stories about swimming

1304
00:52:55.200 --> 00:52:57.060
at the New York Athletic Club.

1305
00:52:57.060 --> 00:52:58.080
Yeah, a strange place.

1306
00:52:58.080 --> 00:52:58.980
Back in the day.

1307
00:52:59.940 --> 00:53:02.100
So I would stand there.
It's a weird place.

1308
00:53:02.100 --> 00:53:04.260
Yeah, very, but I would stand there,

1309
00:53:04.260 --> 00:53:08.070
and I'd see someone like you, or Olympics swimmers,

1310
00:53:08.070 --> 00:53:10.410
like, I mean you name it, I've seen everyone there

1311
00:53:10.410 --> 00:53:12.240
Michael Phelps, everyone.

1312
00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:13.897
And I would see someone who looked good, and I'd be like,

1313
00:53:13.897 --> 00:53:15.870
"Hey buddy, do me a favor, will you?

1314
00:53:15.870 --> 00:53:17.250
Watch me swim down and back, and tell

1315
00:53:17.250 --> 00:53:18.660
me what I'm doing wrong."

1316
00:53:18.660 --> 00:53:20.587
Most people, if you ask for help are like,

1317
00:53:20.587 --> 00:53:22.096
"Yeah, all right, go for it."

1318
00:53:22.096 --> 00:53:24.270
And you pick up a tidbit here and there,

1319
00:53:24.270 --> 00:53:25.627
then you see the guy the next day I'm like,

1320
00:53:25.627 --> 00:53:29.130
"Hey, am I doing this right?"

1321
00:53:29.130 --> 00:53:31.110
So when people will send me DMs and be like,

1322
00:53:31.110 --> 00:53:32.028
how do I do this?

1323
00:53:32.028 --> 00:53:32.861
How do I do that?

1324
00:53:32.861 --> 00:53:34.950
I'm like, brother, if you really wanna do this,

1325
00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:37.470
you don't need to ask anyone else, everything's available,

1326
00:53:37.470 --> 00:53:38.880
you can find YouTube.

1327
00:53:38.880 --> 00:53:39.960
If you really wanna do it,

1328
00:53:39.960 --> 00:53:43.020
you don't need me to tell you, you need to just do it.

1329
00:53:43.020 --> 00:53:44.370
Get in the pool and start swimming.

1330
00:53:44.370 --> 00:53:46.410
It's like running, I just started running.

1331
00:53:46.410 --> 00:53:49.050
I didn't have a game plan, I was running 10 miles a day,

1332
00:53:49.050 --> 00:53:51.960
and I started to recognize that I have good fitness,

1333
00:53:51.960 --> 00:53:53.640
I bet I can win some races.

1334
00:53:53.640 --> 00:53:57.810
But back to the swimming, I was just trying so hard.

1335
00:53:57.810 --> 00:54:00.420
And I think that the people who were the regular swimmers

1336
00:54:00.420 --> 00:54:02.940
there at the time that I was swimming, they respected it.

1337
00:54:02.940 --> 00:54:04.560
They were like, "Oh, this guy wants to get better."

1338
00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:07.920
And then people wanna help you if you wanna help yourselves,

1339
00:54:07.920 --> 00:54:10.110
just like rehab, or recovery,

1340
00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:12.060
if you see someone who's trying hard,

1341
00:54:12.060 --> 00:54:13.860
and looks like they need help, but they're putting

1342
00:54:13.860 --> 00:54:16.320
in the work, and not asking for anything,

1343
00:54:16.320 --> 00:54:18.360
people started to try to help me,

1344
00:54:18.360 --> 00:54:21.930
and very quickly I got strong enough in the swim

1345
00:54:21.930 --> 00:54:25.500
that I could at least not be blown out the back.

1346
00:54:25.500 --> 00:54:27.780
So when I got on the bike in a non drafting race,

1347
00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:30.633
I could make up some work, and then do work on the run.

1348
00:54:33.883 --> 00:54:35.520
If I could swim a little bit better,

1349
00:54:35.520 --> 00:54:37.860
we can progress into it, but I eventually came in

1350
00:54:37.860 --> 00:54:40.890
like six to seventh that Ironman Wisconsin overall,

1351
00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:44.970
same at Ironman, 70.3, in Honu, in Kona,

1352
00:54:44.970 --> 00:54:46.233
the half Ironman.

1353
00:54:47.610 --> 00:54:50.460
And every time I was like, oh, all the time I lost

1354
00:54:50.460 --> 00:54:52.140
in the swim is the difference between winning

1355
00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:54.210
the overall and losing.

1356
00:54:54.210 --> 00:54:55.110
Swimming is tough though,

1357
00:54:55.110 --> 00:54:57.780
because if you don't learn it at a young age,

1358
00:54:57.780 --> 00:54:59.673
you can get up to a certain point.

1359
00:55:00.810 --> 00:55:02.970
It's like learning a language when you're young,

1360
00:55:02.970 --> 00:55:05.640
it just becomes so much harder when you're older.

1361
00:55:05.640 --> 00:55:07.650
And the guys who are just, they have that technique,

1362
00:55:07.650 --> 00:55:09.660
it's like the fitness is almost secondary.

1363
00:55:09.660 --> 00:55:12.540
If you have that efficient, smooth technique in the pool,

1364
00:55:12.540 --> 00:55:16.080
it's beautiful, and you can do countless hours of work

1365
00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:17.050
without damaging your body.

1366
00:55:17.050 --> 00:55:18.930
Well, it's all about efficiency,

1367
00:55:18.930 --> 00:55:20.760
and making the water work for you.

1368
00:55:20.760 --> 00:55:24.810
And people that don't kind of have that level of technique,

1369
00:55:24.810 --> 00:55:27.150
are fighting the water all the time.

1370
00:55:27.150 --> 00:55:29.760
Not only are they going way slower, they're expending twice

1371
00:55:29.760 --> 00:55:32.400
the amount of energy exactly, and in Ironman,

1372
00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:36.210
you just wanna be in contact with the group or whatever,

1373
00:55:36.210 --> 00:55:39.810
so it's about holding back as much as it is anything else.

1374
00:55:39.810 --> 00:55:41.520
So when you're getting out of the water,

1375
00:55:41.520 --> 00:55:43.590
you're fighting to be part of that.

1376
00:55:43.590 --> 00:55:46.380
And you're like tapped to some level,

1377
00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:47.467
and those guys are like,

1378
00:55:47.467 --> 00:55:49.560
"I'm good to go on the bike now."

1379
00:55:49.560 --> 00:55:50.730
No, that's exactly right.

1380
00:55:50.730 --> 00:55:53.070
And inevitably what would happen is I'd get on the bike,

1381
00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:54.783
and start trying to make it all up in the first

1382
00:55:54.783 --> 00:55:57.360
like 40 miles, and by the time I got off the bike,

1383
00:55:57.360 --> 00:55:59.670
I was like, I'm on death tour.

1384
00:55:59.670 --> 00:56:01.380
And you're doing this without any coaching,

1385
00:56:01.380 --> 00:56:03.720
or really any program, like you're just going out,

1386
00:56:03.720 --> 00:56:04.980
I'm gonna run 10 miles every day.

1387
00:56:04.980 --> 00:56:07.890
Like that's not exactly like the best strategy

1388
00:56:07.890 --> 00:56:11.010
for getting these goals done.

1389
00:56:11.010 --> 00:56:12.120
Are you going online and saying,

1390
00:56:12.120 --> 00:56:14.280
well, here's how you prepare for an Ironman,

1391
00:56:14.280 --> 00:56:16.320
should look like this, or are you just trying

1392
00:56:16.320 --> 00:56:19.650
to do it the Ken way.

1393
00:56:19.650 --> 00:56:22.770
A combination of both, but I would argue that

1394
00:56:22.770 --> 00:56:24.600
with the running the 10 miles a day,

1395
00:56:24.600 --> 00:56:25.890
I'd argue that that does work.

1396
00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:27.570
How many people are actually gonna do that?

1397
00:56:27.570 --> 00:56:31.050
Like that's all I did, and I started running really fast.

1398
00:56:31.050 --> 00:56:31.923
No days off?

1399
00:56:33.630 --> 00:56:35.250
When I lived out here, the only time I would take

1400
00:56:35.250 --> 00:56:38.190
a day off was to do like a 50 to 100 mile bike ride

1401
00:56:38.190 --> 00:56:39.270
with Rob Moore.

1402
00:56:39.270 --> 00:56:41.610
So I'd do my long run on Saturday,

1403
00:56:41.610 --> 00:56:45.180
a long bike ride on Sunday typically, or vice versa.

1404
00:56:45.180 --> 00:56:48.390
But with the Ironman, I eventually hired Jimmy Riccitello,

1405
00:56:48.390 --> 00:56:51.960
who's the head ref at Ironman, and a really nice guy,

1406
00:56:51.960 --> 00:56:54.120
and he helped me really get ready.

1407
00:56:54.120 --> 00:56:55.710
That's a legend.
Yeah.

1408
00:56:55.710 --> 00:56:58.620
So in 2012, so I'm doing all these little races,

1409
00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:00.390
and I'm getting better, and I'm learning things here

1410
00:57:00.390 --> 00:57:02.820
and there, and I'm trying to pick up things from everyone.

1411
00:57:02.820 --> 00:57:07.230
And in 2012, I qualified for Kona and Ironman, New York.

1412
00:57:07.230 --> 00:57:10.950
And to me, I had achieved like a dream that I didn't have

1413
00:57:10.950 --> 00:57:13.500
it for years, but when I saw Ironman on TV, I was like,

1414
00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:16.770
that is unbelievable, wow, that looks so compelling,

1415
00:57:16.770 --> 00:57:18.960
it's impossible to do, but wow.

1416
00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:21.510
If those people can do it, why can't I, why not me?

1417
00:57:22.650 --> 00:57:25.500
I had always had the kind of an attitude of like,

1418
00:57:25.500 --> 00:57:27.060
I don't know that I can do that,

1419
00:57:27.060 --> 00:57:28.017
what these other people are doing.

1420
00:57:28.017 --> 00:57:29.797
And that was really the first time where I was like,

1421
00:57:29.797 --> 00:57:32.190
"No, dude, you are only limited by what you think.

1422
00:57:32.190 --> 00:57:34.290
Like, if those people can do it, you can do this."

1423
00:57:34.290 --> 00:57:36.690
And I convinced myself, I could do it, and I did,

1424
00:57:36.690 --> 00:57:40.830
and I qualified with a roll down spot in New York.

1425
00:57:40.830 --> 00:57:42.120
For those who don't know, there's a certain amount

1426
00:57:42.120 --> 00:57:44.250
of age group spots at each Ironman race,

1427
00:57:44.250 --> 00:57:46.170
and Ironman, New York, because it was the inaugural race,

1428
00:57:46.170 --> 00:57:47.640
they had a couple extra spots,

1429
00:57:47.640 --> 00:57:50.040
and let's say there's 10 spots,

1430
00:57:50.040 --> 00:57:52.740
and if someone of the 10 doesn't take their spot,

1431
00:57:52.740 --> 00:57:55.020
it rolls to the next to the 11th place finisher

1432
00:57:55.020 --> 00:57:56.460
in that age group.

1433
00:57:56.460 --> 00:57:59.430
So I got a roll down spot in New York, and I qualified,

1434
00:57:59.430 --> 00:58:01.500
now, I like, "Yes, we're going to Hawaii."

1435
00:58:01.500 --> 00:58:03.810
I had two little kids, my two little babies,

1436
00:58:03.810 --> 00:58:06.645
and me and my wife, and we rented a house on Ali'i Drive.

1437
00:58:06.645 --> 00:58:08.645
I was like, "I'm going to the Olympics."

1438
00:58:10.661 --> 00:58:11.910
I was like, "Man, I did it."

1439
00:58:11.910 --> 00:58:14.460
I was on a high, and I get there, and it's just like

1440
00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:17.490
you would imagine my equivalent of the Olympic village,

1441
00:58:17.490 --> 00:58:20.370
it was like all the like triathlon nerds, myself included,

1442
00:58:20.370 --> 00:58:22.920
where all like everyone's running around, happy to be there,

1443
00:58:22.920 --> 00:58:26.280
it's like such a culty place.

1444
00:58:26.280 --> 00:58:28.890
And everyone's crazy fit, everywhere you look.

1445
00:58:28.890 --> 00:58:31.980
You're like crazy guys looks a lot fitter than I look.

1446
00:58:31.980 --> 00:58:34.770
Compression socks, veins everywhere.

1447
00:58:34.770 --> 00:58:38.610
So we get there and same thing, swim, I suffer through it,

1448
00:58:38.610 --> 00:58:40.860
get on the bike, start working way too hard,

1449
00:58:40.860 --> 00:58:43.680
full of irrational exuberance, and sure enough,

1450
00:58:43.680 --> 00:58:46.950
I get off the bike and I'm like, oh my God, I'm crippled.

1451
00:58:46.950 --> 00:58:48.930
I can barely move.

1452
00:58:48.930 --> 00:58:51.330
And my wife's at like mile three on Ali'i Drive,

1453
00:58:51.330 --> 00:58:53.340
and I'm like hobbling running down, and I mean,

1454
00:58:53.340 --> 00:58:56.790
I feel terrible, and I see her and she's videotaping,

1455
00:58:56.790 --> 00:58:58.230
and it's almost like with tears in my eyes,

1456
00:58:58.230 --> 00:59:01.898
I'm giving her the like throat cut sign, like cut, cut,

1457
00:59:01.898 --> 00:59:05.037
like a director, like don't film me.

1458
00:59:05.037 --> 00:59:07.493
And as soon as I got there, I'm like, I can't do it,

1459
00:59:08.428 --> 00:59:12.240
I'm stepping off, I'm gonna walk back to the transitionary,

1460
00:59:12.240 --> 00:59:14.430
I'm not in a good spot physically.

1461
00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:17.520
And I almost had this mentality of like,

1462
00:59:17.520 --> 00:59:18.810
almost like a professional, like, wow,

1463
00:59:18.810 --> 00:59:19.860
if I'm not gonna be in the money,

1464
00:59:19.860 --> 00:59:22.770
like I told myself every story under the sun

1465
00:59:22.770 --> 00:59:24.338
to justify quitting,

1466
00:59:24.338 --> 00:59:27.180
I'm not gonna be in the top of my age group,

1467
00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:28.590
I'm not gonna do this, I'm not,

1468
00:59:28.590 --> 00:59:30.810
like basically, I was just a quitter.

1469
00:59:30.810 --> 00:59:34.500
I just packed it in like oh, I'm so disgusted.

1470
00:59:34.500 --> 00:59:37.110
Even in hindsight thinking about it now makes me mad,

1471
00:59:37.110 --> 00:59:40.440
but I did, I just quit, and I had no excuse

1472
00:59:40.440 --> 00:59:41.690
other than I was hurting.

1473
00:59:44.316 --> 00:59:47.700
It was such a letdown, I went from the highest high

1474
00:59:47.700 --> 00:59:51.030
in terms of like my personal satisfaction with myself,

1475
00:59:51.030 --> 00:59:53.850
to like really depressed.

1476
00:59:53.850 --> 00:59:56.160
Like, oh man, I am, I just felt

1477
00:59:56.160 --> 00:59:59.220
so ashamed isn't the right word, but it was almost

1478
00:59:59.220 --> 01:00:00.754
like a level of shame internally.

1479
01:00:00.754 --> 01:00:03.630
And my wife had been super supportive of me training

1480
01:00:03.630 --> 01:00:05.268
for this 'cause you know it takes like a ton of time.

1481
01:00:05.268 --> 01:00:06.101
Unbelievable amount time.

1482
01:00:06.101 --> 01:00:09.090
I've got two babies, she's like, "What are you doing?"

1483
01:00:09.090 --> 01:00:10.320
After this we're done.

1484
01:00:10.320 --> 01:00:11.700
Yeah, exactly.

1485
01:00:11.700 --> 01:00:13.470
And I was like, yeah, we had like a deal,

1486
01:00:13.470 --> 01:00:16.170
she had like written up a contract like you agree

1487
01:00:16.170 --> 01:00:17.196
that I'll support you to a contract.

1488
01:00:17.196 --> 01:00:18.900
She wrote you a contract?

1489
01:00:18.900 --> 01:00:20.040
I think she wrote it for the second one.

1490
01:00:20.040 --> 01:00:20.950
Good for her.

1491
01:00:22.287 --> 01:00:25.170
But my wife is like, when it comes to like being

1492
01:00:25.170 --> 01:00:28.920
a good partner, and a mom, she's world class,

1493
01:00:28.920 --> 01:00:30.090
she's the world record holder,

1494
01:00:30.090 --> 01:00:32.880
she's as good as anyone I've ever seen.

1495
01:00:32.880 --> 01:00:35.520
She knows, she's awesome.

1496
01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:37.710
And she was supportive just like I was her fifth child,

1497
01:00:37.710 --> 01:00:39.930
which is like what it feels like most of the time,

1498
01:00:39.930 --> 01:00:43.080
'cause she's the one that has like the rational brain

1499
01:00:43.080 --> 01:00:45.570
when I get irrational and emotional.

1500
01:00:45.570 --> 01:00:47.100
So I said afterwards, we're sitting there,

1501
01:00:47.100 --> 01:00:49.471
and I'm like so depressed, I might even have been crying,

1502
01:00:49.471 --> 01:00:52.890
and I was like, "I know I can do better than that."

1503
01:00:52.890 --> 01:00:55.530
She's like, "If it's that important, do it next year,

1504
01:00:55.530 --> 01:00:57.090
just do it again, and you know,

1505
01:00:57.090 --> 01:00:58.500
now you have to qualify again.

1506
01:00:58.500 --> 01:01:01.200
It's not just like, oh, just sign up next year."

1507
01:01:01.200 --> 01:01:02.370
So I was like, "Really?"

1508
01:01:02.370 --> 01:01:04.590
And she was like, "If it's that important you do it."

1509
01:01:04.590 --> 01:01:06.260
And I was like, "I'm gonna do everything

1510
01:01:06.260 --> 01:01:07.650
in my power to win."

1511
01:01:07.650 --> 01:01:12.330
And I qualified at Ironman either Whistler or Wisconsin,

1512
01:01:12.330 --> 01:01:16.410
Whistler, and went back and did like a 9:39.

1513
01:01:16.410 --> 01:01:18.240
And I was so proud of myself.

1514
01:01:18.240 --> 01:01:20.190
I was like, that's a respectable time, man.

1515
01:01:20.190 --> 01:01:21.900
I would've liked to have been on the 9:30,

1516
01:01:21.900 --> 01:01:24.180
but I was like, yes, I did it.

1517
01:01:24.180 --> 01:01:27.603
And yeah, she supported everything through that.

1518
01:01:28.620 --> 01:01:30.990
Then she said, "Let me draw your attention

1519
01:01:30.990 --> 01:01:33.096
to section six clause A."

1520
01:01:33.096 --> 01:01:34.494
(Ken laughs)

1521
01:01:34.494 --> 01:01:36.501
Yep, pretty much.

1522
01:01:36.501 --> 01:01:41.501
And then like any addict, I couldn't stop this addiction

1523
01:01:42.480 --> 01:01:44.310
to endurance sports in triathlon.

1524
01:01:44.310 --> 01:01:48.630
And I did go back to Kona one more time in 2016,

1525
01:01:48.630 --> 01:01:51.060
when I moved out here, I qualified at Wisconsin,

1526
01:01:51.060 --> 01:01:54.210
which is the one that I could have won that if I had swam,

1527
01:01:54.210 --> 01:01:56.760
I didn't, we had had our fourth child, and I just was like,

1528
01:01:56.760 --> 01:01:58.440
I'm not even gonna swim, I'm not gonna swim.

1529
01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:01.175
To save five minutes it's gonna take me like two hours,

1530
01:02:01.175 --> 01:02:04.410
three times a week, I'll just take the 1:10,

1531
01:02:04.410 --> 01:02:06.720
which is what I swam, and then I rode like a 4:58,

1532
01:02:06.720 --> 01:02:09.840
and ran a 3:15, and finished like seventh overall.

1533
01:02:09.840 --> 01:02:11.850
Won my age group, and maybe sixth,

1534
01:02:11.850 --> 01:02:14.460
and went back to Kona in 16, ad I was like,

1535
01:02:14.460 --> 01:02:16.710
oh, I'm gonna get him, I'm gonna like win my age group,

1536
01:02:16.710 --> 01:02:18.930
or be in the money in 16.

1537
01:02:18.930 --> 01:02:21.600
And I got pneumonia like three weeks before the race,

1538
01:02:21.600 --> 01:02:25.500
and went back, and walked the entire marathon.

1539
01:02:25.500 --> 01:02:28.432
But after having quit in 13, and then gone back there,

1540
01:02:28.432 --> 01:02:30.690
I was like, I know I'm not coming back here unless,

1541
01:02:30.690 --> 01:02:33.000
so I got on the run, and I was just suffering I was like,

1542
01:02:33.000 --> 01:02:34.050
that's okay, I'll just walk.

1543
01:02:34.050 --> 01:02:36.270
I'm not gonna disrespect myself, or the race.

1544
01:02:36.270 --> 01:02:38.470
And I just walked and I made it like a click

1545
01:02:40.470 --> 01:02:42.300
either right over under 11 hours,

1546
01:02:42.300 --> 01:02:43.590
but right before the sun went down,

1547
01:02:43.590 --> 01:02:45.890
I was like, oh, thank God, I'm racing the sun.

1548
01:02:47.490 --> 01:02:49.080
And it was around that time that I started

1549
01:02:49.080 --> 01:02:52.770
to just do more running just because of a lack of time.

1550
01:02:52.770 --> 01:02:54.300
And that's when I started the 10 miles

1551
01:02:54.300 --> 01:02:56.160
every day around 2016.

1552
01:02:56.160 --> 01:02:56.993
Right.

1553
01:02:56.993 --> 01:02:59.493
(upbeat music)

1554
01:03:02.490 --> 01:03:06.413
You got really far just on grit, and determination,

1555
01:03:06.413 --> 01:03:11.413
and persistence like here's what I'm doing in kind

1556
01:03:11.580 --> 01:03:14.220
of a bullheaded way, like I don't need to really look

1557
01:03:14.220 --> 01:03:16.350
and see what else anybody's doing,

1558
01:03:16.350 --> 01:03:18.393
I know me, and I can figure this out.

1559
01:03:19.440 --> 01:03:22.110
But in order to really level up and achieve your goals,

1560
01:03:22.110 --> 01:03:24.120
you did reach that point where you're like,

1561
01:03:24.120 --> 01:03:25.770
hey, I need to bring somebody in.

1562
01:03:27.190 --> 01:03:29.220
And I think there's a lot of people who are watching,

1563
01:03:29.220 --> 01:03:31.470
or listening to this, who can relate to that

1564
01:03:31.470 --> 01:03:33.960
like I can't afford a coach, or I have a job,

1565
01:03:33.960 --> 01:03:36.510
and like I just go out, and do what I can do every day.

1566
01:03:36.510 --> 01:03:39.750
So I think it would be cool to share a little bit about

1567
01:03:39.750 --> 01:03:41.940
like what you've learned through those relationships

1568
01:03:41.940 --> 01:03:45.210
that could be helpful to the amateur athletes out there.

1569
01:03:45.210 --> 01:03:47.490
I would say this, if you are spending time,

1570
01:03:47.490 --> 01:03:49.710
and effort doing this stuff, I would argue that

1571
01:03:49.710 --> 01:03:51.180
you can't afford not to have a coach,

1572
01:03:51.180 --> 01:03:53.100
because everyone has so much time, and money,

1573
01:03:53.100 --> 01:03:54.840
and effort invested into their result,

1574
01:03:54.840 --> 01:03:56.550
if it's something important to them.

1575
01:03:56.550 --> 01:03:58.680
If you're just doing weekend races and trying to finish,

1576
01:03:58.680 --> 01:04:00.240
like maybe you don't need a coach,

1577
01:04:00.240 --> 01:04:02.610
but if you wanna be competitive, even if it's with yourself.

1578
01:04:02.610 --> 01:04:03.720
Your approach, you're an amateur,

1579
01:04:03.720 --> 01:04:05.790
but you train like a professional now.

1580
01:04:05.790 --> 01:04:08.700
Do you treat it like any professional athlete would?

1581
01:04:08.700 --> 01:04:10.590
Oh, if my wife tells me like, what are you doing?

1582
01:04:10.590 --> 01:04:12.540
We're going ski, we were in Aspen, we're skiing,

1583
01:04:12.540 --> 01:04:13.980
I'm like, yeah, I gotta work for us.

1584
01:04:13.980 --> 01:04:14.813
She's like, "What do you mean?"

1585
01:04:14.813 --> 01:04:15.960
I'm like, "well, this is my job."

1586
01:04:15.960 --> 01:04:19.140
Like this is part of who I am.

1587
01:04:19.140 --> 01:04:22.350
I need this to feel, I need this to start my day.

1588
01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:26.090
And I would say about a coach like if it's really important

1589
01:04:26.090 --> 01:04:28.020
to you, and it could be just you racing yourself,

1590
01:04:28.020 --> 01:04:30.090
maybe you wanna break 3:30, whatever it is,

1591
01:04:30.090 --> 01:04:33.750
if you think that you've invested this kind of time

1592
01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:35.100
and effort to hire a coach, I mean, there's coaches,

1593
01:04:35.100 --> 01:04:38.280
and it's super expensive, and there's tons of online assets.

1594
01:04:38.280 --> 01:04:40.380
But one of the things that I found in working

1595
01:04:40.380 --> 01:04:42.540
with real coaches, and guys who are very knowledgeable,

1596
01:04:42.540 --> 01:04:44.280
especially like Jimmy Riccitello,

1597
01:04:44.280 --> 01:04:46.140
when it comes to triathlon and cycling, I mean,

1598
01:04:46.140 --> 01:04:48.090
his son is like now a pro tour rider,

1599
01:04:48.090 --> 01:04:50.783
I think he might even be on a pro tour team in Europe,

1600
01:04:53.820 --> 01:04:56.340
Jimmy gave me my introduction to like real structure,

1601
01:04:56.340 --> 01:04:58.650
and real accountability more than anything.

1602
01:04:58.650 --> 01:05:00.780
Is like, I could look at online plans,

1603
01:05:00.780 --> 01:05:03.090
but if there's no one kind of monitoring it,

1604
01:05:03.090 --> 01:05:06.870
even if it's just not that I was trying to impress someone,

1605
01:05:06.870 --> 01:05:07.703
but just knowing that-

1606
01:05:07.703 --> 01:05:09.540
He's gonna look at it.

1607
01:05:09.540 --> 01:05:10.590
You don't wanna let him down.

1608
01:05:10.590 --> 01:05:13.110
I viewed Mario and Jimmy, and I talked to Mario

1609
01:05:13.110 --> 01:05:15.750
all the time Jimmy, I talked occasionally,

1610
01:05:15.750 --> 01:05:18.390
but not since I'm not doing triathlon much less.

1611
01:05:18.390 --> 01:05:19.837
But when I talk to Mario, I tell him,

1612
01:05:19.837 --> 01:05:24.030
"Dude, we're partners, we're teammates, I'm representing you

1613
01:05:24.030 --> 01:05:25.590
when I'm out there, I believe this.

1614
01:05:25.590 --> 01:05:27.390
I believe I only have one teammate in running,

1615
01:05:27.390 --> 01:05:29.760
it's me and you, it's me, or with Mario."

1616
01:05:29.760 --> 01:05:32.400
And I'm like, "You write the formula,

1617
01:05:32.400 --> 01:05:33.450
you write the prescription,

1618
01:05:33.450 --> 01:05:34.710
and I'm gonna take the medicine."

1619
01:05:34.710 --> 01:05:37.170
So he gives structure, and like you said,

1620
01:05:37.170 --> 01:05:40.110
I was running 10 miles a day, and when I got to 2:33,

1621
01:05:40.110 --> 01:05:42.420
I couldn't break through, I went back to CIM,

1622
01:05:42.420 --> 01:05:43.833
and ran like 2:35.

1623
01:05:44.970 --> 01:05:47.130
But running a 1:10 in the first half,

1624
01:05:47.130 --> 01:05:49.440
probably wasn't very conducive to running fast

1625
01:05:49.440 --> 01:05:51.930
for the second half, considering that was like three minutes

1626
01:05:51.930 --> 01:05:54.390
faster than my half marathon PR.

1627
01:05:54.390 --> 01:05:56.490
The year after I ran 2:28, I was like,

1628
01:05:56.490 --> 01:05:57.900
oh, I bet I can run 2:26.

1629
01:05:57.900 --> 01:06:00.675
So I ran 1:10 first half, and it blew myself outta

1630
01:06:00.675 --> 01:06:03.330
the water with three or four miles to go.

1631
01:06:03.330 --> 01:06:05.550
But when it comes to the running with Mario,

1632
01:06:05.550 --> 01:06:07.979
it's like he writes the plan, and I follow it

1633
01:06:07.979 --> 01:06:10.320
like a roadmap.

1634
01:06:10.320 --> 01:06:14.190
Meaning if there's traffic on one direction,

1635
01:06:14.190 --> 01:06:17.160
I might take a slightly different, but ultimately I'm trying

1636
01:06:17.160 --> 01:06:18.300
to get to the same place.

1637
01:06:18.300 --> 01:06:20.550
So the coaching is like a rough outline,

1638
01:06:20.550 --> 01:06:22.170
it's not an exact science.

1639
01:06:22.170 --> 01:06:23.730
Like today, there was a track workout,

1640
01:06:23.730 --> 01:06:26.670
but obviously I was got in late, didn't sleep great,

1641
01:06:26.670 --> 01:06:28.560
So I said, I'm gonna do that tomorrow on the beach.

1642
01:06:28.560 --> 01:06:30.240
And I just make changes here and there,

1643
01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:32.130
but it's literally like those kind of things

1644
01:06:32.130 --> 01:06:35.220
we don't even have to talk about because he sees everything

1645
01:06:35.220 --> 01:06:37.140
that I'm doing in real time, and everyone can,

1646
01:06:37.140 --> 01:06:38.657
I post everything on Strava.

1647
01:06:39.780 --> 01:06:41.670
And part of that is for accountability too.

1648
01:06:41.670 --> 01:06:45.153
It's like this is what I do.

1649
01:06:47.790 --> 01:06:52.140
I get a lot out of it, that accountability provides me

1650
01:06:52.140 --> 01:06:55.740
with structure, and a sense of accomplishment

1651
01:06:55.740 --> 01:06:59.130
to start the day, it's like the military guy who was given

1652
01:06:59.130 --> 01:07:00.787
the commencement address, and he said,

1653
01:07:00.787 --> 01:07:02.460
"Start the day by making your bed,

1654
01:07:02.460 --> 01:07:03.690
you have one win already."

1655
01:07:03.690 --> 01:07:06.480
That's how I view the run is, if nothing else happens today,

1656
01:07:06.480 --> 01:07:08.880
I've taken care of my most important asset,

1657
01:07:08.880 --> 01:07:10.800
my mental and physical health.

1658
01:07:10.800 --> 01:07:13.980
It's more important to me than everything it has to be.

1659
01:07:13.980 --> 01:07:18.060
I can't be my best self with my children, or my wife,

1660
01:07:18.060 --> 01:07:20.220
and I'm useless to anyone if I'm not healthy.

1661
01:07:20.220 --> 01:07:24.630
And part of this exercise every day in this 10 mile therapy,

1662
01:07:24.630 --> 01:07:27.360
is as much mental health as it is physical.

1663
01:07:27.360 --> 01:07:31.170
It makes me feel good, it is my antidepressant,

1664
01:07:31.170 --> 01:07:33.840
when I go through periods of not being able to do it,

1665
01:07:33.840 --> 01:07:36.270
like when I had the shoulder surgery, I had a few days

1666
01:07:36.270 --> 01:07:37.533
of real depression,

1667
01:07:38.880 --> 01:07:40.290
I don't wanna say suicidal thoughts,

1668
01:07:40.290 --> 01:07:43.313
but certainly like suicidal ideation of like,

1669
01:07:43.313 --> 01:07:45.690
would I be better off just not doing this?

1670
01:07:45.690 --> 01:07:47.070
Do I wanna be here?

1671
01:07:47.070 --> 01:07:50.370
And I know when I say it it sounds irrational,

1672
01:07:50.370 --> 01:07:51.930
and I reflect on it, it sounds crazy,

1673
01:07:51.930 --> 01:07:55.980
but I can't deny that these are the thoughts that enter

1674
01:07:55.980 --> 01:07:59.370
my head when I'm not holding myself accountable,

1675
01:07:59.370 --> 01:08:02.160
and maybe it's a blessing, maybe it's a curse,

1676
01:08:02.160 --> 01:08:06.360
but this is just the reality of my life is I just,

1677
01:08:06.360 --> 01:08:07.890
maybe from the mistakes I've made,

1678
01:08:07.890 --> 01:08:11.100
and the things that I've been through,

1679
01:08:11.100 --> 01:08:13.602
like this it's just what I need.

1680
01:08:13.602 --> 01:08:17.190
I'm not opposed to hearing professional takes.

1681
01:08:17.190 --> 01:08:18.900
So I'm like, you should try this, this, this.

1682
01:08:18.900 --> 01:08:22.470
I'm not close-minded, but right now that's my state of mind,

1683
01:08:22.470 --> 01:08:23.580
is I need this.

1684
01:08:23.580 --> 01:08:26.820
Yeah, in hearing you share that,

1685
01:08:26.820 --> 01:08:30.660
I certainly relate to that deeply, but at the same time,

1686
01:08:30.660 --> 01:08:34.560
I also can't help but think like you're holding

1687
01:08:34.560 --> 01:08:35.975
onto it so tightly.

1688
01:08:35.975 --> 01:08:39.090
Like on some level like is it self-flagellation

1689
01:08:39.090 --> 01:08:42.150
like you're doing penance, like you're punishing yourself

1690
01:08:42.150 --> 01:08:45.411
because there's a sense of undeservedness,

1691
01:08:45.411 --> 01:08:49.320
or you're not worthy until, or unless you get that in.

1692
01:08:49.320 --> 01:08:53.070
And there's gotta be moments when those priorities come

1693
01:08:53.070 --> 01:08:57.720
into conflict when your workout is at odds

1694
01:08:57.720 --> 01:09:01.200
with something that you need to do with your kids,

1695
01:09:01.200 --> 01:09:03.390
and you have to make those decisions around

1696
01:09:03.390 --> 01:09:05.730
where you're gonna invest your time, and your energy.

1697
01:09:05.730 --> 01:09:07.710
When there's real conflict, I will say like,

1698
01:09:07.710 --> 01:09:12.710
I will make the amendments, and adjustments on the fly.

1699
01:09:12.720 --> 01:09:15.030
Like if someone had an eight o'clock little league game,

1700
01:09:15.030 --> 01:09:17.400
and it was like, there's so many kids,

1701
01:09:17.400 --> 01:09:20.040
we divide and conquer, but if there was one that we all have

1702
01:09:20.040 --> 01:09:21.690
to be at, like I'll go to the game,

1703
01:09:21.690 --> 01:09:23.730
and then maybe I'll run home, or I'll do something,

1704
01:09:23.730 --> 01:09:24.840
but I'll always like-

1705
01:09:24.840 --> 01:09:25.680
Find a way to work it out.

1706
01:09:25.680 --> 01:09:28.620
I try to find that balance, but you are not far off there

1707
01:09:30.060 --> 01:09:31.140
in your assessment there.

1708
01:09:31.140 --> 01:09:33.300
There's definitely like some things that I know

1709
01:09:33.300 --> 01:09:36.540
that I need to address. I don't profess

1710
01:09:36.540 --> 01:09:40.830
to have all the answers, or to be doing everything right,

1711
01:09:40.830 --> 01:09:42.863
I know that there's things I need to improve.

1712
01:09:42.863 --> 01:09:43.860
I think there's a gift in there

1713
01:09:43.860 --> 01:09:46.320
if you're willing to explore it.

1714
01:09:46.320 --> 01:09:48.630
I'm not a professional, I don't know what it's about,

1715
01:09:48.630 --> 01:09:52.180
I mean, perhaps it has something to do with a sense

1716
01:09:53.070 --> 01:09:55.470
of guilt, like you have this amazing life,

1717
01:09:55.470 --> 01:09:58.020
and this is just the payment that you have to make

1718
01:09:58.020 --> 01:10:02.370
on a daily basis to earn, or deserve the life that you have.

1719
01:10:02.370 --> 01:10:04.650
I don't know, and there's certainly nothing wrong

1720
01:10:04.650 --> 01:10:07.320
with being competitive, and hard driving,

1721
01:10:07.320 --> 01:10:09.630
and you're accomplishing all of these things,

1722
01:10:09.630 --> 01:10:11.880
and then you share it so openly with people,

1723
01:10:11.880 --> 01:10:12.960
and that's of service.

1724
01:10:12.960 --> 01:10:15.660
And so I don't mean to malign any of it,

1725
01:10:15.660 --> 01:10:17.790
I just think it's interesting and curious,

1726
01:10:17.790 --> 01:10:20.910
and I think there is like some kind

1727
01:10:20.910 --> 01:10:23.700
of like growth opportunity for you there.

1728
01:10:23.700 --> 01:10:25.980
You're not maligning me, and I don't feel

1729
01:10:25.980 --> 01:10:29.807
at all uncomfortable, I think part of the benefit

1730
01:10:31.110 --> 01:10:33.360
of doing something like this is being able to share

1731
01:10:33.360 --> 01:10:35.370
my truths, and my reality with people,

1732
01:10:35.370 --> 01:10:38.460
and hoping that somehow I can improve the quality

1733
01:10:38.460 --> 01:10:40.860
of my own life through sharing my stories.

1734
01:10:40.860 --> 01:10:44.700
And you're 100% right, I know that this isn't necessarily

1735
01:10:44.700 --> 01:10:47.070
like the greatest success story in history.

1736
01:10:47.070 --> 01:10:48.720
